Let $\alpha$ be a simple root, $P_\alpha$ its minimal parabolic, $Y=G/P_\alpha$, and $p_\alpha:X=G/B	o Y$. Let $\kappa\in\mathfrak h^*$ satisfy $\langle\kappa,\alpha^\veeangle=0$. The restriction of $\kappa$ to $\mathfrak p_\alpha$ that is zero on root spaces is a Lie algebra character. Define the partial-flag TDO $\mathcal D^{\alpha}_{\kappa-ho}$ on $Y$ as the action-algebroid quotient whose isotropy character is this restriction of $\kappa$; the subscript $\kappa-ho$ uses the convention that a TDO parameter $
u$ has isotropy character $
u+ho$. For a quasicoherent left $\mathcal D^{\alpha}_{\kappa-ho}$-module $N$, define $p_\alpha^\dagger N$ on a Zariski product chart $p_\alpha^{-1}(U)\cong U	imes\mathbb P^1$ to be the exterior product $\mathcal D_{\mathbb P^1} oxtimes N|_U$, with its transition maps induced by changes of the $P_\alpha$-torsor section. Then these local modules glue to a quasicoherent left $\mathcal D_{\kappa-ho}$-module, and tensoring by $\mathcal O_X(ho)$ turns it into a left $\mathcal D_\kappa$-module. The restriction of $\mathcal O_X(ho)$ to each $p_\alpha$-fiber is $\mathcal O_{\mathbb P^1}(-1)$. For a torsor section $s:U	o G$, the product chart used below is the commutative square whose top arrow is $\Phi_s(u,qB)=s(u)qB$:  egin{center}  egin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth] 
ode (a) at (0,1.3) {$U	imes P_\alpha/B$}; 
ode (b) at (4,1.3) {$p_\alpha^{-1}(U)$}; 
ode (c) at (0,0) {$U$}; 
ode (d) at (4,0) {$U$}; \draw[->] (a) -- node[above] {$\Phi_s$} (b); \draw[->] (a) -- node[left] {$\mathrm{pr}_1$} (c); \draw[->] (b) -- node[right] {$p_\alpha$} (d); \draw[->] (c) -- node[below] {$\mathrm{id}_U$} (d); \end{tikzpicture} \end{center} The left vertical map is projection, and the bottom map is identity. The torsor section makes $\Phi_s$ an isomorphism; the equality $p_\alpha\circ\Phi_s=\mathrm{pr}_1$ verifies commutativity. The diagram records the local chart only; the PBW and gluing arguments below prove the TDO identifications.
